---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'install' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Install the extension"
  description="Install the extension from a ready-to-load release archive, with no build tools, or directly from source. Then authorize the domains where you review, or deploy it across an organization.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / install</p>
          <h1>Install the extension</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">There are two ways to install the extension today: a ready-to-load release archive, or a build from source. A store listing will follow. You can try the extension without installing Node, Git, or any other development tools.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>From your browser's extension store — coming soon</h2>
          <p>The extension hasn't been submitted to the Chrome Web Store or Firefox Add-ons yet, so there is no store listing to install from today. Until those listings are available, use the release archive described below.</p>
          <div class="callout warn">
            <span class="ct">why this matters — and not just for convenience</span>
            <h4>Store-installed extensions update automatically.</h4>
            <p>An extension loaded manually from a ZIP stays on that version until you update it yourself. For an individual trial, that may be fine. For a team, it can quickly mean different people running different versions without realizing it. That's why the store installation is listed here even though it isn't available yet: it will be the recommended distribution method for teams.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>From a release ZIP — no Node, no Git</h2>
          <p>This is the simplest option for a locked-down machine where installing a development toolchain isn't possible. Every tagged release includes two ready-to-load archives: one for Chromium-based browsers and one for Firefox.</p>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Download the archive for your browser</b><span class="tx">Go to the <a href="https://github.com/Reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">releases list</a> — use the full list rather than <code>/releases/latest</code>, because the latter does not include prereleases. Download either <code>…-chromium.zip</code> or <code>…-firefox.zip</code>.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Extract it somewhere permanent</b><span class="tx">Don't leave the extracted extension in your Downloads folder. Chromium-based browsers load the extension from that folder whenever they start. If you move or delete it later, the extension will stop working.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>Chrome, Edge, Brave, and other Chromium-based browsers</b><span class="tx">Open <code>chrome://extensions</code>. Enable <b>Developer mode</b>, choose <b>Load unpacked</b>, then select the extracted folder that directly contains <code>manifest.json</code>.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">4</span><b>Firefox</b><span class="tx">Open <code>about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox</code>. Choose <b>Load Temporary Add-on</b>, then select the <code>manifest.json</code> file itself — not the folder. Firefox removes temporary add-ons when the browser closes.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <div class="callout blk">
            <span class="ct">one important limitation</span>
            <h4>An extension installed this way does not update automatically.</h4>
            <p>This method is ideal for trying the extension or for machines where you can't install the build toolchain. It is not intended as a long-term distribution channel. You will remain on the version you installed until you repeat the process manually. For team-wide deployment, use the browser stores once the listings are available.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>From source</h2>
          <p>Use this method if you want to inspect the code you're running or contribute to the project.</p>
          <pre class="code">git clone https://github.com/reefact/conventional-comments-toolkit
cd conventional-comments-toolkit

npm ci
npm run build:extension     <span class="tk-cm">// → packages/extension/dist-ext</span></pre>
          <p>Then load <code>dist-ext</code> using the instructions for your browser: step 3 above for Chromium-based browsers, or step 4 for Firefox. The Firefox version is built at the same time in its own subfolder.</p>
          <p class="small">Requires Node 20 or later. The repository also includes a step-by-step Windows/PowerShell guide covering everything from installing Node to loading the extension in your browser.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Authorize the domains where you review</h2>
          <p>A freshly installed extension is active nowhere. It has no permanent access to any site: you grant the extension access to each domain one at a time, and can withdraw it the same way. The options page opens by itself the first time to say so, and offers a guided tour you can skip at any point or replay later.</p>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Open the extension's settings</b><span class="tx">The page opens on its own right after installation. You can reopen it at any time from the extension icon in your browser toolbar.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Activate a domain the extension already knows</b><span class="tx">github.com, Azure DevOps Services (<code>dev.azure.com</code>) and GitLab.com each have a card of their own: one click asks your browser for that domain and, once you approve, records which platform serves it — so you never have to classify these yourself.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>Add any other domain, and select its platform</b><span class="tx">A GitHub Enterprise Server instance, a GitHub Enterprise Cloud domain on <code>ghe.com</code>, or an internal host that only serves your organization's configuration is typed in by hand. The extension can't tell from a name like <code>git.acme.internal</code> what is running there. Selecting the platform tells it which adapter to use — or that the host serves configuration and nothing else.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">4</span><b>Approve the browser permission</b><span class="tx">Your browser asks whether the extension may access that domain. You decide whether to grant it. Every domain you have allowed then carries a “Withdraw” button on the options page, and your browser's own controls still revoke it too.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small">Authorizing a domain does not widen what the extension supports. On Azure DevOps it works on <code>dev.azure.com</code> only: an Azure DevOps Server, or an organization still on <code>visualstudio.com</code>, is not covered, and granting access to it changes nothing. The same goes for GitLab Self-Managed and GitLab Dedicated, which are not supported yet. The <a href={href('platforms', 'en')}>platforms page</a> has the full picture.</p>
          <div class="callout warn">
            <span class="ct">if you are updating from 1.0.0-beta.10 or earlier</span>
            <h4>The extension goes quiet on github.com until you allow it.</h4>
            <p>Earlier versions carried a built-in permission on github.com, active from the moment you installed the extension and revocable from nowhere inside it. That domain now goes through the same one-click authorization as every other, and no update can grant it on your behalf: a browser only gives a site permission after an explicit action from you. The options page opens by itself to say so — but only if the update leaves you with no working domain at all. If you already had another one configured, the extension keeps working there, and nothing announces that github.com has gone silent.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Check that it works</h2>
          <p>Open any pull request on a domain you have authorized and start writing a review comment. A row of labels should appear above the comment box, with a status pill below it. Type <code>?i</code>, then press <code>Tab</code>. It should expand to <code>issue:</code>.</p>
          <p>The settings page shows the exact version you are running next to its title — <code>v1.0.0-rc.2</code>, for example — where <code>chrome://extensions</code> shows <code>1.0.0</code> for every prerelease. Quote that version when you report a problem.</p>
          <p>If nothing appears, the <a href={href('troubleshooting', 'en')}>troubleshooting page</a> covers the most common causes. On GitLab, check which editor you are using first: the extension works in the plain-text editor, not in the rich text one.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Deploy it across an organization</h2>
          <p>Everything above is done by one person, on one machine. An organization that wants the same setup everywhere uses its browsers' enterprise policy instead: the same channel installs the extension and carries its settings.</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li><strong>Force the installation.</strong> The policy is called <code>ExtensionInstallForcelist</code> on Chrome and Edge, and <code>ExtensionSettings</code> with <code>"installation_mode": "force_installed"</code> on Firefox. A forced installation needs a source the browser can update from: the public stores once the listings exist, or a private store that your organization runs.</li>
            <li><strong>Authorize the domains for everyone.</strong> <code>allowedHosts</code> lists the domains authorized in advance, each with its platform — for example <code>&#123;"host": "ghes.example.corp", "platform": "github"&#125;</code>, or <code>"platform": "config"</code> for a host that only serves the organization's configuration. Nobody has to go through the settings page for these, and nobody can withdraw them from it.</li>
            <li><strong>Set the floor.</strong> <code>floor</code> carries the minimum mode, the rules that no repository may relax, and the address of the organization's shared configuration. The <a href={href('configure', 'en')}>configuration page</a> explains how the three levels combine.</li>
            <li><strong>Declare a telemetry endpoint, if you want one.</strong> Telemetry is off by default and can only be made available through this policy: <code>telemetry.enabled</code> set to <code>true</code> and an <code>https</code> address in <code>telemetry.endpoint</code>. Each person must still tick the box on their own settings page before anything is sent. The <a href={href('privacy', 'en')}>privacy page</a> says what the counters contain.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small">These three settings live under the extension's managed storage, which browsers fill from the policy and which the extension can only read. A key that the extension does not declare there is dropped by the browser before it arrives.</p>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
